# Intro
A single star at the center of a uniform, periodic glass-IC gas box, with
photoelectric heating and LW/FUV hyperbolic propagation on (photoionization
and radiation pressure off), same as `../ISRFHyperbolicPropagation/`. The
star's mass is chosen so it reaches the end of its main-sequence lifetime
partway through the run: this example checks that the LW/FUV field it built
up decays away afterwards instead of persisting at its last injected level.
`isrf_source_turn_off_check.py` tracks the box-total mass-weighted FUV/LW
specific energy over time, marks the star's death time (computed from its
mass and metallicity, not read off any snapshot field), and checks the
post-death decay against the absorption timescale `1/(c_hyp*kappa)`.

Unlike `../ISRFHyperbolicPropagation/`, this example leaves
`LW_FUV_dissipation_alpha_max`/`LW_FUV_dissipation_alpha_floor`/
`LW_FUV_c_hyp_margin` unset in `params.yml`, so it runs at GEARFeedback's own
shipped defaults (see `parameter_example.yml`) rather than isolating pure
propagation.

## Snapshot cadence
The field builds up over the star's few-Myr lifetime, then decays on a much
shorter absorption timescale once the star dies, so a single fixed
`delta_time` cannot resolve both. `Snapshots:output_list` instead lists a
handful of coarse points early on and a fine, closely-spaced run starting
slightly before the star's expected death through the decay that follows.
The shipped `output_list_isrf_source_turn_off.txt` is tied to `star_mass`
and `initial_metallicity` (they set the death time) and to `gas_density`,
`gas_mass` and `dt_max` (they set the post-death decay timescale, through
`c_hyp = C_hyp*h/dt`); `level` only sets the box size. `time_end` was chosen
as `t_death + 15*tau_FUV` and must not be shortened below the output list's
last entry. Regenerate the list if you change any of the coupled parameters
materially: fine spacing about `tau_LW/4`, from `0.85*t_death` to
`t_death + 15*tau_FUV`, then phase-shifted so no entry falls within
`2*dt_max` of `t_death` (a snapshot that close to the switch-off step reads
as ambiguous, neither clearly pre- nor post-death). Take `t_death` and the
e-fold times from `isrf_source_turn_off_check.py`'s own printed output on a
run with the new parameters.

## Metallicity
As in `../ISRFHyperbolicPropagation/`: the dust screening/absorption length
scales with metallicity relative to Grackle's solar value, so
`initial_metallicity` must stay above 0. Defaults to solar (`Z/Zsun=1`).

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```
`--with-tracers=GEAR` is required: the tracers module registers the
`FUVSpecificEnergies`/`LWSpecificEnergies` snapshot output.

# Run
```
./run.sh
```
Override any parameter via the matching env var -- gas density
(`gas_density`, atom/cm^3), gas particle mass (`gas_mass`, Msun), star mass
(`star_mass`), star type (`star_type`), resolution level (`level`, N =
`(2**level)**3` gas particles), run duration and stepping (`time_end`,
`dt_max`), metallicity (`initial_metallicity`), and thread count
(`n_threads`); see `run.sh` for the full list and defaults. `run_name=<dir>
./run.sh` moves the run's outputs into `<dir>` once done.

# Checking
```
python3 isrf_source_turn_off_check.py
```
`run.sh` runs this automatically, last, against the final run's own
snapshots (repointed at `<dir>` if `run_name` was set; running the check
manually against a moved run needs `--snapshot`, `--timesteps-log` and
`--used-parameters` repointed the same way). It prints the star's
mass/metallicity/death time, then three things:
- the measured post-death e-folding time of each band against the analytic
  `1/(c_hyp*kappa)` estimate -- informational only, not a pass/fail gate;
- the residual field fraction left at `time_end` relative to the pre-death
  level -- **gated**;
- whether any particle's field went negative after death, excluding
  snapshots within `--negativity-settle-myr` of death (the switch-off
  transient, reported separately, not scored) -- **gated**. A FAIL
  confined to the switch-off transient is expected and not a problem; a
  FAIL in a later, scored snapshot indicates a real issue.

Produces a plot of both bands' total energy vs. time (`--output`, default
`isrf_source_turn_off_check.png`).
